ite multiplayer on  november 15th just days before  battlefield 2042 launch ea  communications vice president john  riesberg told pc gamer that the stories  floating around the internet do not  accurately represent the quote  discussion and the context of the town  hall meeting reesberg said this was an  in-depth and very humble internal  conversation about battlefield 2042's  launch the xfire report which was  published by tom henderson said  battlefield 2042 might have struggled in  the market due to development issues  with its engine the work from home setup  due to covid and comparisons to other  more polished games in the market like  halo infinite but now let's talk about  battlefield's other competitor call of  duty activision has provided an update  on call of duty's anti-cheat ricochet  which was launched in 2021 amid  complaints that warzone had been overrun  with cheaters in a blog post activision  said cheating within warzone reached an  all-time low during the holidays  speaking to the success of ricochet's  tools however fighting cheaters is a  never-ending project and activision  acknowledged this in its statement the  company also said it has released a new  feature called damage shield which can  identify cheating in real time and  prevent the player from doing critical  damage to other players this system will  never impact law abiding players damage  shield is one of many new features that  make up ricochet and more are in  development since some of the features  rolled out in the past few weeks  activision said it saw a decline in  cheating reports activision also teased  what sounds like potentially more  outlandish or unexpected cheating  mitigation efforts  it said quote you might start seeing  clips of our new tricks soon but we  won't ruin the surprise the company said  it wants to share as much detail as it  can regarding anti-qi projects but it  can't and won't always do this so as to  not give cheaters a heads up but all  right it's frid